WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.589
Film Courage: Markus regarding connections,
I have a quote here (I don’t know who said

00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:10.230
it) “Connections are good but nobody is
going to put their business in jeopardy because

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.230
of a connection.”

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.370
Markus Redmond, Writer/Director/Actor: That’s
absolutely true.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.590
Film Courage: Did you say it?

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:16.700
Markus: No…no…wait…did I?

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:17.750
No I didn’t.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:24.210
Film Courage: I don’t know where we got
this quote.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.950
Markus: It’s true though.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:31.340
It’s a business, especially now.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:40.760
And it’s become a very risk-adverse business,
especially now because people don’t like

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.899
going to the movie theater unless SPIDER-MAN
is there.

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:55.909
So studios are spending the money where the
money is going to get spent.

00:00:55.909 --> 00:01:07.590
So you can know Lorenzo di Bonaventura (one
of the guys who runs Warner Brothers) and

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:12.140
you can be like “Hey man, I know you are
doing a lot of stuff right now.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:16.799
I’ve got this musical about socks.”

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:20.210
Lorenzo is probably going to say “You keep
that to yourself.”

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:21.210
You know what I mean?

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:28.240
It’s a business and it’s shifting daily
especially now with all of the streaming wars

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.530
going on and everyone is upset with Netflix.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:37.140
They are pulling their content off of Netflix
and starting their own steamers and they are

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.520
trying to brand themselves.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.010
This is what we do.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:43.060
This is what we don’t do.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.341
Counter-branding against that to get their
own.

00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:49.179
A lot of money at stake.

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:51.320
A lot of it’s business.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.799
If what you do lines up with their business
model, cool.

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:57.189
It doesn’t mean you’re not talented.

00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:01.229
It doesn’t mean that what you do isn’t
great.

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:03.710
It means it doesn’t fit them.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:13.320
I just had a pitch meeting that went amazing
at major place.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:21.020
And the executives were totally into what
we had done and they thought it was really

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.670
funny and timely.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.320
But they are rebranding.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.870
Their brand is this.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:32.560
My stuff was this.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.040
So they are like “It’s great.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.550
The writing is great.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.410
It’s going to be a fun show.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.240
It’s not for us.”

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:48.950
And that’s fine because everybody is doing
what they need to do for their business.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:57.310
I don’t advocate doing or changing your
stuff to fit somebody’s business.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:04.340
But it doesn’t hurt to sort of pay attention
to what network, studios, cablers, streamers.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:12.450
What are they doing and what do I have in
my arsenal.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.030
Where does this fit?

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.810
Where does this make sense?

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.510
You know what I mean?

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:19.950
Because you’re in business too.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.620
Your business is getting your brand out there.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.970
And there is somewhere for every brand.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.210
You’ve got a brand, they’ve got a brand.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:32.490
You’ve just got to find a brand that links
up.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:38.110
So that’s my very long winded answer to
that question.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:45.960
Film Courage: So when someone says “Now
I’m mad because Person B won’t help me

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.880
get to Person A and now I’m mad and they’re
just trying to hold me down and this industry

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:50.880
is no good.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.220
Everybody is just out for themselves…”

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:58.220
Is that really it or is it that we all have
our own name and reputation at stake, “I’d

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:01.280
love to help you but…”

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:11.690
Markus: For that person, yes it’s true because
they’ve made a decision to see it that way,

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.530
for them it’s true.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:16.760
It’s absolutely true.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:24.750
The great thing about being a human being
with a brain is you get to decide how you

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:25.970
see things.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:32.020
You get to choose how you react to what happens
to you.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.600
So you can decide “Well this is a bummer.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.450
It’s never going to happen.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:38.930
Everybody bums me out.”

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:45.610
You can take the script, put it in a box under
your bed, walk around and be mad.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:57.100
Or you can say “Okay, those are two people
in an industry with thousands of people and

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:02.480
some how my work made me a film.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.650
Which means it probably will make me another
film.”

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:16.250
So I guess what I can do keep going and find
another spot, another avenue, another outlet,

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:23.890
another way to get this material that I believe
into the right hands.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:29.670
The right hands is super important because
it’s not just about getting the thing set

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.070
up, getting the thing sold.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:35.930
You can get the thing set up, get the thing
sold and if it’s in the wrong hands, it’s

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.150
going to be crap.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:46.280
You need to be able to lock in with whoever
is going to be working with you to bring the

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:48.090
thing to fruition.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:54.120
That’s what makes a film or a show great
because everybody (for the most part) gets

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.120
on the same page.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:58.600
There are always disagreements “Well I think
this should happen.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.660
I think this character should be here and
not there.”

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.620
It’s just part of it.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:10.370
But if the overall goal is the same, that’s
what you want.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:20.080
So if Person B cannot take you to Person A
(or will not take you to Person A), it’s

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.320
not the right fit.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:29.810
There’s a good fit that is out there, it’s
just not this person.

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:35.240
And this person might be a better fit for
that other thing you’ve got so make sure

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.000
you’ve got another thing.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:46.100
I tell writers all of the time that if you
want to get cooking and you’ve got a script

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:51.720
you dig, that’s awesome.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:56.840
You should never stop, but you should have
something else.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:04.530
Because again brains, people could be like
“I’m not into this but I like the writing.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:09.670
What else you got?”And then if you don’t
have anything else and they are just sitting

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:13.100
there…remember the attention span is very
short.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.020
So if you can’t come back and say “Oh
yeah, I’ve got another thing.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.419
Why don’t we talk next week about the new
thing?”

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:26.300
People forget, people see a lot of people,
especially executives.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:32.910
The scripts they have to read, the pitches
they hear, all of the continual stuff coming

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.010
at them.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:43.980
It’s very, very rare for these guys to remember
this one specific thing that I saw three months

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.520
ago.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:53.490
They can, it happens if it really stood out
to that one person but you know…better your

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.490
odds.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:59.960
Just keep making content, keep working (I
think).

